WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.575
Decision science helps people make clearer, more explicit,

00:00:03.575 --> 00:00:06.030
and more reviewable choices under uncertainty.

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:07.940
It is not a promise of perfect prediction.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:11.800
It is a discipline for improving the quality of the decision before the outcome is known,

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:13.800
then learning from what happens afterward.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.802
Strong decision practice starts with the frame.

00:00:15.802 --> 00:00:17.920
Define the real choice, not just the symptom.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.540
Clarify the scope, constraints, stakeholders, time horizon, decision owner,

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:23.315
and consequences of inaction.

00:00:23.315 --> 00:00:24.950
Then clarify objectives, values,

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:28.610
and criteria so the group knows what it is trying to optimize and what tradeoffs it is

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:29.610
willing to accept.

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:33.480
Good decision makers generate realistic options and avoid false binaries.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:37.465
They compare tradeoffs, opportunity costs, reversibility, and staged commitments.

00:00:37.465 --> 00:00:40.975
They make uncertainty explicit with probabilities, base rates, forecasts,

00:00:40.975 --> 00:00:44.760
confidence ranges, scenario thinking, and updates as evidence changes.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:49.625
They consider risk as likelihood, impact, context, downside, upside, tail exposure,

00:00:49.625 --> 00:00:51.120
mitigation, and contingency.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.010
They also respect the limits of judgment.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:55.960
Bias and noise are normal risks, not personal insults.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:59.965
Checklists, pre-mortems, red teams, independent estimates, decision records,

00:00:59.965 --> 00:01:03.812
and structured review help teams improve without pretending people are perfectly rational.

00:01:03.812 --> 00:01:04.425
In teams,

00:01:04.425 --> 00:01:08.460
role clarity and psychological safety make it more likely that the right information

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.160
reaches the decision maker in time.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.960
The goal is better judgment, better process, better learning,

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:14.995
and more reliable action over time.

00:01:14.995 --> 00:01:17.150
You will still make decisions with incomplete information.

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:21.585
Some good decisions will still have bad outcomes, and some weak decisions will get lucky.

00:01:21.585 --> 00:01:23.680
Decision science gives you a way to keep improving anyway.
